  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret pure real exponentials by setting $s=\\sigma$ in $e^{st}$ and using the sign and magnitude of $\\sigma$ to determine non-oscillatory growth, decay, or constancy over time.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Pure Real Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set $s=\\sigma$ in the generalized exponential $e^{st}$ and you get the pure real exponential $x(t)=e^{\\sigma t}$. Here $t$ is the variable and $\\sigma$ is a fixed real number, so the only thing the signal can do is change in magnitude over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{\\sigma t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $\\sigma=0$ as the base case. Then $e^{0t}=1$, which is the constant unit signal and the cleanest reference point for the sign rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{0t}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $\\sigma<0$, the signal decays toward $0$ as $t$ increases. When $\\sigma>0$, it grows. In both cases the behavior is non-oscillatory because the exponent is real.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma<0 \\Rightarrow x(t)\\to 0,\\qquad \\sigma=0 \\Rightarrow x(t)=1,\\qquad \\sigma>0 \\Rightarrow x(t)\\to \\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The size of $|\\sigma|$ controls how fast the change happens: larger $|\\sigma|$ means faster decay or faster growth. That sign-and-magnitude rule is the core way to read pure real exponentials.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $e^{\\sigma t}$ from $s=\\sigma$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $s=-4$ in $e^{st}$. Rewrite the signal and identify whether it grows, decays, or stays constant.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $s$ is real, write $s=\\sigma$ with $\\sigma=-4$. Substituting that value into $e^{st}$ gives the pure real exponential form in $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $\\sigma=-4<0$, the exponent becomes more negative as $t$ increases, so the signal decays toward $0$. There is no oscillation here because the exponent is real.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma=-4<0,\\qquad x(t)\\to 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The magnitude $|\\sigma|=4$ controls how fast the decay happens, but the sign is what tells you that this signal decays rather than grows or stays constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Decay When $\\sigma<0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{-2t}$, describe the behavior as $t$ increases and explain why the exponent sign forces decay.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a real coefficient, so write $s=\\sigma$. Here $\\sigma=-2<0$, which puts the signal in the decay case right away.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substituting gives $x(t)=e^{-2t}$. As $t$ increases, the exponent becomes more negative, so the signal shrinks toward $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma=-2<0,\\qquad x(t)\\to 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the exponent is real, there is no oscillation. The sign tells you it decays, and the magnitude $|\\sigma|=2$ sets how fast it decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Growth When $\\sigma>0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{3t}$, describe the behavior as $t$ increases and explain why the positive exponent forces growth.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a real coefficient, so write $s=\\sigma$. Here $\\sigma=3>0$, which puts the signal in the growth case right away.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substituting gives $x(t)=e^{3t}$. As $t$ increases, the exponent becomes more positive, so the signal increases without bound and never oscillates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma=3>0,\\qquad x(t)\\to\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the exponent is real, the rise is monotone rather than oscillatory. The sign tells you it grows, and the magnitude $|\\sigma|=3$ sets how fast it grows.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Constancy When $\\sigma=0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set $\\sigma=0$ in $e^{\\sigma t}$ and determine the resulting signal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a real coefficient, so write $s=\\sigma$. Here $\\sigma=0$, which is the boundary case between growth and decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{0t}=e^0=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substituting gives $x(t)=e^{0t}=1$. The value no longer depends on $t$, so the signal stays constant at the unit level.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma=0,\\qquad x(t)=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the exponent is real and equal to zero, there is no oscillation and no growth or decay. The zero exponent produces the constant unit signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Real Exponentials",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Comparing Real Exponential Rates Using $|\\sigma|$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $x(t)=e^{-2t}$ and $y(t)=e^{-5t}$ and decide which one decays faster.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both exponents are real and negative, so both signals are in the decay case. To compare their rates, look at the magnitudes $|\\sigma|$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-2t},\\qquad y(t)=e^{-5t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $|{-5}|>|{-2}|$, so $y(t)$ has the larger magnitude exponent. That means $y(t)$ changes faster in magnitude, so it decays faster than $x(t)$ as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|{-5}|>|{-2}|\\;\\Rightarrow\\; e^{-5t}\\text{ decays faster than }e^{-2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The general rule is that the sign tells you whether the signal grows or decays, while the larger $|\\sigma|$ tells you which one changes faster. In this case, both signals decay, and the one with the larger magnitude decays more quickly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
